Q: OutlineGroup with SwiftData A: <pre><code>To achieve a folder structure similar to Xcode's project navigator with your SwiftData `Entry` model, you'll need to introduce a `Folder` entity, establish relationships between folders and entries, and then use a unified data type (like an enum) to represent both folders and entries in an `OutlineGroup`. Below is a step-by-step guide in English.

---

### 1. Update Your Data Models

Create a `Folder` model that can contain subfolders and entries, and link each entry to an optional folder. Use SwiftData’s `@Model` macro and define the relationships explicitly.

    import SwiftData
    import Foundation

    @Model
    final class Folder {
        var name: String
        @Relationship(deleteRule: .cascade) var subfolders: [Folder]
        @Relationship(deleteRule: .cascade, inverse: \Entry.folder) var entries: [Entry]
        var parentFolder: Folder?   // Inverse relationship for subfolders

        init(name: String) {
            self.name = name
            self.subfolders = []
            self.entries = []
        }
    }

    @Model
    final class Entry {
        var fullName: String
        var folder: Folder?   // Inverse relationship pointing to Folder.entries

        init(fullName: String) {
            self.fullName = fullName
        }
    }

**Key Points**:
- The `deleteRule: .cascade` ensures that deleting a folder also deletes its subfolders and entries (adjust if needed).
- The `parentFolder` property is optional; it helps SwiftData maintain the inverse relationship for subfolders automatically.
- The `folder` property in `Entry` links it to a parent folder (or `nil` for root-level entries).

---

### 2. Create a Unified Type for OutlineGroup

`OutlineGroup` works with a tree of items that each provide a `children` array. Define an enum `FolderItem` that can represent either a `Folder` or an `Entry`. It should conform to `Identifiable` and provide a computed `children` property.

    enum FolderItem: Identifiable {
        case folder(Folder)
        case entry(Entry)

        var id: PersistentIdentifier {
            switch self {
            case .folder(let folder): return folder.id
            case .entry(let entry): return entry.id
            }
        }

        var name: String {
            switch self {
            case .folder(let folder): return folder.name
            case .entry(let entry): return entry.fullName
            }
        }

        var children: [FolderItem]? {
            switch self {
            case .folder(let folder):
                // Combine subfolders and entries, sort by name
                let subfolderItems = folder.subfolders.map { FolderItem.folder($0) }
                let entryItems = folder.entries.map { FolderItem.entry($0) }
                return (subfolderItems + entryItems).sorted { $0.name &lt; $1.name }
            case .entry:
                return nil
            }
        }
    }

---

### 3. Display the Hierarchy in a View

Use `@Query` to fetch all root folders (those without a parent) and root entries (those not assigned to any folder). Combine them into `rootItems` and feed into an `OutlineGroup`.

    import SwiftUI
    import SwiftData

    struct EntriesView: View {
        // Root folders: folders with no parentFolder
        @Query(filter: #Predicate&lt;Folder&gt; { $0.parentFolder == nil })
        private var rootFolders: [Folder]

        // Root entries: entries with no folder
        @Query(filter: #Predicate&lt;Entry&gt; { $0.folder == nil })
        private var rootEntries: [Entry]

        var rootItems: [FolderItem] {
            let folderItems = rootFolders.map { FolderItem.folder($0) }
            let entryItems = rootEntries.map { FolderItem.entry($0) }
            return (folderItems + entryItems).sorted { $0.name &lt; $1.name }
        }

        var body: some View {
            NavigationStack {
                List {
                    OutlineGroup(rootItems, children: \.children) { item in
                        switch item {
                        case .folder(let folder):
                            // Folder row: no NavigationLink, just icon and name
                            HStack {
                                Image(systemName: &quot;folder&quot;)
                                Text(folder.name)
                            }
                        case .entry(let entry):
                            // Entry row: NavigationLink to detail view
                            NavigationLink(value: entry) {
                                HStack {
                                    Image(systemName: &quot;doc&quot;)
                                    Text(entry.fullName)
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
                .navigationDestination(for: Entry.self) { entry in
                    EntryView(entry: entry)
                }
                .navigationTitle(&quot;Entries&quot;)
            }
        }
    }

---

### 4. How It Works

- **Folders**: Because they have children, `OutlineGroup` automatically provides a disclosure indicator (chevron) and toggles expansion when tapped on the row (excluding the chevron). The folder row is just a plain `HStack` – no `NavigationLink` – so tapping the folder’s name/icon will expand/collapse it.
- **Entries**: Without children, `OutlineGroup` does not show a disclosure indicator. We wrap the entry row in a `NavigationLink` that pushes to `EntryView`. The `NavigationLink`’s own disclosure arrow will appear, indicating that tapping the entry navigates to its details.
- **Navigation**: We use `navigationDestination(for: Entry.self)` to handle navigation to the detail view.
- **Sorting**: Inside `children`, we sort subfolders and entries alphabetically by name. You can customize the sorting logic (e.g., folders first, then entries).

---

### 5. Next Steps

- **Adding/Editing Data**: You’ll need to write code to create new folders and entries, assigning them to the appropriate parent folder (or `nil` for root).
- **Persisting Expansion State**: `OutlineGroup` manages expansion state internally, but if you need to remember which folders are open across app launches, you’ll have to implement a custom recursive list with `DisclosureGroup` and store state manually.
- **Performance**: `OutlineGroup` is lazy and works well for moderately sized trees.

This setup gives you a folder hierarchy exactly like Xcode’s project navigator, where folders can contain subfolders and entries, and tapping an entry opens its detail view.
</code></pre>

Q: How to make a list of objects visible in Unity Inspector? A: <p>First of all I would recommend a <a href="https://docs.unity3d.com/6000.3/Documentation/Manual/class-ScriptableObject.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>ScriptableObject</code></a> for this kind of profile things</p>
<pre><code>[CreateAssetMenu] // &lt;- can be further customized via parameters
public class WeaponProperties : ScriptableObject
{
    // your fields

    // NO constructor
}
</code></pre>
<p>This allows you to create assets via right click in the Project folder -&gt; Create -&gt; <code>WeaponProperties</code>. Then you configure each asset according to your needs.</p>
<p>Then in your class you simply expose a list/array where you can drag in/select your according profile assets</p>
<pre><code>[SerializeField] private WeaponProperties[] _availableWeapons;
</code></pre>
<p>and then you would simply go e.g. by index</p>
<pre><code>[SerializeField] private int _currentSelectedIndex;
</code></pre>
<p>and if you want can expose it directly as</p>
<pre><code>public WeaponProperties CurrentSelectedWeapon =&gt; _availableWeapons[_currentSelectedIndex];
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>If you rather want some meaningful fix weapon IDs you can indeed use an <code>enum</code>. In that case though I would recommend <a href="https://github.com/ayellowpaper/SerializedDictionary" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>SerializedDictionary</code></a> whdich would allow you to directly serialize a map <code>enum -&gt; WeaponProperties</code></p>
<pre><code>public enum WeaponType
{      
    Pitola,
    Rifle
}

[SerializeField] private SerializedDictionary&lt;Weaponype, WeaponProperties&gt; _availableWeapons;
[SerializeField] private WeaponType _currentSelectedType;

public WeaponProperties CurrentSelectedWeapon =&gt; _avaialableWeapons[_currentSelectedType];
</code></pre>

Q: Why is my struct destructed twice with `std::variant` and `std::monostate`? A: <p>This line:</p>
<pre><code>ab = A();
</code></pre>
<p>Is creating a temporary <code>A</code> object, and then moving it into <code>ab</code>.<br />
You can observe this by adding copy/move constructors and assignment operators:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;variant&gt;

struct A
{
    A()                    { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Constructing A\n&quot;; }
    A(A const&amp;)            { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Copy constructing A\n&quot;; }
    A(A&amp;&amp;)                 { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Move constructing A\n&quot;; }
    A&amp; operator=(A const&amp;) { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Copy assigning A\n&quot;; return *this; }
    A&amp; operator=(A&amp;&amp;)      { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Move assigning A\n&quot;; return *this; }
    ~A()                   { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Destructing A\n&quot;; }
};

struct B
{
};

int main()
{
    std::variant&lt;std::monostate, A, B&gt; ab;
    ab = A();
}
</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre><code>Constructing A
Move constructing A
Destructing A
Destructing A
</code></pre>
<p><kbd><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/qhasGs5Eq" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Live demo 1</a></kbd></p>
<p>You can avoid the copy/move, by using <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/utility/variant/emplace" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>std::variant::emplace</code></a>.<br />
If you replace the above mentioned line with:</p>
<pre><code>ab.emplace&lt;A&gt;();
</code></pre>
<p>The output should become:</p>
<pre><code>Constructing A
Destructing A
</code></pre>
<p><kbd><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/d6vWTPrMT" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Live demo 2</a></kbd></p>
